  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Coastal low to bring increased rain chances on \nSunday.\n\nThe current setup at the surface features high pressure\nretreating into New England. To the south, an inverted trough \nremains along the southeastern U.S. coastline while a wavy \nfrontal zone stretches across central Florida to the north of \nthe Bahamas. Over the next 12 to 24 hours, the persistent ridge\npulls into the Canadian Maritimes. At the same time, the \nfrontal system around Florida is slated to lift northward, \ngenerally paralleling the Atlantic coastline. How close the \naccompanying wave(s) of embedded low pressure track will \ndictate the level of local impacts to the region. The latest\ntrend is for several disorganized low centers to consolidate\ninto a more cohesive system a bit further out to sea.\n\nWinds are gradually shifting to easterly/onshore as the \ninverted coastal trough approaches. Any rain chances look to \nhold off until late tonight, perhaps longer given dry low-level \nprofiles to start.\n\nLatest 12Z guidance has come into better agreement keeping the\nsteadiest rain east of the area. Will continue to forecast a \nbroad 0.10 to 0.25 inches over the area (perhaps slightly less\nin the Shenandoah Valley), perhaps accompanied by a few pockets\napproaching 0.50 inches toward the Chesapeake Bay. With a more \noffshore track of the series of lows, the strength of north to \nnortheasterly winds remains limited, as well. Outside of the \nrain chances, high temperatures will mainly be in the mid 50s to\nmid 60s on Sunday.\n\nThe forecast looks dry for the start of the new work week as an\nupstream cold front moves through early Monday. A downsloping \nnorthwesterly wind will still help raise temperatures well into \nthe 60s. This also produces a favorable upslope flow to produce \nsome light showers along/west of the Allegheny Front. East of\nthe mountains, waves of stratocumulus clouds are possible. Wind\ngusts may persist at times into Monday night behind a secondary\ncold front that ushers in much cooler air.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler temperatures next week with precipitation \narriving for the Thanksgiving holiday.\n\nBehind multiple cold fronts, temperatures on Tuesday cool and return \nto late-November normals throughout the week. High pressure builds \nover the Ohio River Valley giving our area a brief break in \nrainfall until Wednesday evening.\n\nModels are in good agreement for an elongated trough to dig into the \nMississippi River Valley Wednesday into Thursday. This sets up\nanother coastal low pressure system to trail up the Mid-Atlantic \ncoastline by Thanksgiving. This far out, both deterministic and \nensemble guidance show a wide range of potential solutions, so it's \nfar too early to get into specific details. Most of the solutions \ndiffer in how far this system will pass off the coast. A closer \ntrack would increase precipitation amounts and lead to gustier \nwinds. At higher elevations, mixed wintry precipitation remains a \npossibility Thanksgiving Day, especially across the Alleghenies.\nIf this were to occur, snowfall amounts remain modest with \nrecent guidance trending rainier.\n\nUnless a deeper closed low develops, this system advances into \nthe Atlantic by Friday. High pressure developes in the wake of this \nsystem, bringing seasonable, dry conditions for Friday.",
